# How to update {{SITE_DOMAIN}} (no coding needed)

You describe the change in plain language, an AI assistant makes it, you check it on a
preview link, and you click one button to publish. You never need a terminal, and
nothing you do can affect the live site before you have seen it working.

## One-time setup

1. **A GitHub account** with access to `{{REPO}}`. Ask {{OWNER_NAME}} to add you.
2. **An AI assistant connected to that repository.** Either:
   - **Claude Code on the web** — <https://claude.ai/code> (these instructions assume it)
   - **Google Jules** — <https://jules.google.com>, if you prefer Gemini

   The steps below are the same with either one.

## Making a change

**1. Open <https://claude.ai/code> and select the `{{REPO}}` repository.**

**2. Describe what you want, in normal language.** You don't need to know where anything
lives — it will find it. For example:

- "On the About page, change the second paragraph to say ..."
- "Add this photograph to the Products page" (attach the image)
- "Update the contact email across the whole site to ..."
- "Fix the typo 'recieve' wherever it appears"

**3. Ask it to open a pull request.** A pull request is simply a proposed change waiting
for your approval — the live site is not affected yet.

**4. Open the pull request on GitHub and wait a couple of minutes.** A comment will
appear with a **preview link** — a private copy of the entire site with your change
applied. Click it, and check the result on both a computer and a phone.

**5. Happy with it?** Press the green **Merge pull request** button, then **Confirm
merge**. The live site updates itself within about two minutes.

**6. Not happy?** Tell the assistant what to adjust — the same preview link updates
itself. Or just close the pull request, and nothing happens at all.

## If something looks wrong after publishing

Every published change can be undone with one click. Open the merged pull request on
GitHub, press **Revert**, and merge the revert the same way. The site goes back to
exactly how it was.

## Things that are normal, not faults

- **The contact form doesn't work on a preview link.** Deliberate — previews are not
  allowed to send real email. It works on the live site.
- **A preview link won't open.** Some office networks block `pages.dev` addresses.
  The quickest check is a phone on mobile data — if it opens there, it's your office
  network, not the site. Ask whoever runs the network to allow `*.pages.dev`.
  Changing your DNS to 8.8.8.8 is often suggested for this and usually does **not**
  help: the block is normally on the address name itself, not on looking it up.
- **The preview takes a couple of minutes to appear.** The site is rebuilt from scratch
  each time. If nothing has appeared after five minutes, something failed — ask the
  assistant to check.

## Rules worth knowing

The assistant reads this project's `AGENTS.md` file and follows the house rules by
itself, but these matter enough to know yourself:

{{LANG_NOTE}}

**Don't let it invent facts.** If you ask for something like "add a line about our
experience", check what it writes. Numbers, certifications, standards, client names and
awards must be real. Search engines and regulators both notice, and a wrong
specification is a commercial problem rather than a typo.

{{SITE_RULES}}

